    <h2 id="s1">The short answer</h2>
    <p>A heli-ski week is a rhythm: an arrival day to settle in, several ski days built around helicopter access to untracked terrain, and a departure day. Days are active and exhilarating; evenings are comfortable and restful at a four-star lodge. The whole thing is handled for you, so your only real job is to ski and enjoy it.</p>
    <p>In Iceland the week is based at the <strong>Sigló Hótel</strong> in Siglufjörður, with skiing on the <strong>Troll Peninsula</strong>. Heliski Travel arranges every part of it, and as the authorised agent, booking through us costs exactly the same as going direct. Here is how a week unfolds.</p>

    <h2 id="s2">Arrival day</h2>
    <p>The week usually begins with travel and settling in rather than skiing. You make your way to <strong>Siglufjörður</strong>, typically via a flight into Keflavík and onward travel north through Akureyri, and check into the Sigló Hótel. It is a gentle start by design: you arrive rested rather than rushing onto the mountain jet-lagged.</p>
    <p>The evening brings a <strong>welcome briefing and a gear check</strong>: how the week will run, the safety procedures, the fitting of any provided equipment, and a first look at the forecast. You go to bed knowing exactly what the morning holds. Our guide to <a href="/field-notes/getting-to-north-iceland-heliskiing">getting to North Iceland</a> covers the journey in detail.</p>

    <h2 id="s3">A typical ski day</h2>
    <p>The first full ski day sets the pattern for the rest. It begins with breakfast and a <strong>weather and avalanche briefing</strong>, followed by a check of your BCA safety kit. When the guide gives the word, the helicopter lifts your small group to the first drop.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The descent</strong>: the guide leads a long, untracked run, reading the snow and picking the line.</li>
      <li><strong>The pickup</strong>: the helicopter meets you at the bottom and flies you back up for the next run.</li>
      <li><strong>Mountain lunch</strong>: a pause somewhere spectacular before the afternoon's skiing.</li>
      <li><strong>Home</strong>: a final run and a flight back to the lodge as the day winds down.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>A typical day delivers <strong>15,000 to 25,000 vertical feet across 7 to 14 runs</strong>, all on snow no one else has touched. For a closer look at that first day's nerves and highs, see <a href="/field-notes/what-to-expect-first-heliski-day">what to expect on your first heli day</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="s4">The middle of the week</h2>
    <p>By the second and third ski days, the rhythm is familiar and you relax into it. This is often when the best skiing happens: you know the routine, your legs are tuned to the terrain, and the guide has a clear sense of your group's ability and appetite, so the lines get better and better.</p>
    <p>The middle of the week is also when the flexibility of the format shows. If the group is skiing strongly, the guide can seek out more committing terrain across the <strong>eleven mapped zones</strong>; if a rest is wanted, the pace can ease. Each day is shaped to the group in front of the guide.</p>

    <h2 id="s5">Down days</h2>
    <p>Weather is part of every heli week, and a good trip plans for it rather than pretending it away. On a <strong>down day</strong>, when the mountains are unsafe or unflyable, your skiing is not lost. Because trips are priced by <strong>guaranteed vertical feet</strong> rather than by the day, your vertical carries over to a flyable window instead of evaporating.</p>
    <p>Guides put down days to good use, briefings, local exploration, rest, or a short window if one opens, and fly the moment conditions allow. A longer week simply has more windows, which is why it delivers so reliably. Read more on <a href="/field-notes/weather-and-down-days-heliskiing">weather and down days</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="s6">The evenings</h2>
    <p>Evenings are a genuine part of the week, not an afterthought. After a day on the mountain you fly back over the fjord and step into the <strong>four-star Sigló Hótel</strong>: a proper dinner, the next day's briefing, and downtime in the harbour-side hot tub or a wander through the town.</p>
    <p>The contrast between wild days and comfortable nights is a big part of what makes a heli week feel effortless. You ski hard, then recover properly, ready to do it again. See more on <a href="/field-notes/the-heliski-lodge-experience">the lodge experience</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="s7">Departure day</h2>
    <p>The final morning is usually a departure, with travel back south to Keflavík for your flight home, though many guests build in a buffer day to explore more of Iceland. There is often a quiet satisfaction to this last day: legs tired in the best way, a camera full of untracked lines, and the sense of having done something genuinely rare.</p>
    <p>Because the journey north and south is scenic in its own right, the travel days bookend the skiing with more of Iceland's drama. The <a href="/travel-here">travel page</a> has the practical detail for planning your departure.</p>

    <h2 id="s8">Three, four or five days</h2>
    <p>Heli weeks are built as <strong>three, four and five-day packages</strong>, and the choice comes down to time, budget and weather appetite. A longer week gives the sky more chances to cooperate and makes better use of your travel to North Iceland.</p>
    <p>Most guests find a four or five-day trip the sweet spot: enough days to guarantee good skiing even with a down day or two, and enough time to settle into the rhythm. A three-day trip is a fine shorter option if time is tight. See how the formats and lengths compare in <a href="/field-notes/iceland-heliskiing-packages-compared">Iceland packages compared</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="s9">How the vertical model shapes the week</h2>
    <p>The single detail that shapes a heli week most is how it is priced. Iceland trips are sold by <strong>guaranteed vertical feet</strong>, not by the day. That changes the psychology of the whole week: a storm is a scheduling problem, not a financial one, because your vertical is protected.</p>
    <p>It means you can relax into the week rather than anxiously watching the forecast, knowing that the skiing you paid for will be delivered when the mountains allow. It is the quiet foundation that makes a longer week feel like a safe, sensible choice. For the full explanation, see <a href="/field-notes/what-is-guaranteed-vertical-feet">what guaranteed vertical feet means</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="s10">How to plan it</h2>
    <p>Planning your week is simple with the details handled. Tell Heliski Travel your skiing level, your group, your preferred dates and how long you would like, and we will build the week around you and arrange it end to end. As the <strong>authorised booking agent for Viking Heliskiing</strong>, booking through us costs exactly the same as going direct, and we reply within 12 hours.</p>
    <p>When you are ready, <a href="/packages">browse the packages</a>, see the terrain on the <a href="/iceland">Iceland page</a>, or <a href="/contact">request a tailored quote</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="s11">The verdict</h2>
    <p>A heli-ski week is a beautifully simple rhythm once you see it laid out: arrive and settle, ski untracked mountains by day, recover in comfort by night, and let the guaranteed-vertical model take the weather stress off your shoulders. It is active, exhilarating and, thanks to how it is organised, remarkably relaxing.</p>
    <p>If that is the kind of week you have been picturing, it is exactly what a trip to Iceland delivers. When you are ready, <a href="/packages">browse the packages</a> or <a href="/contact">request a quote</a>. We reply within 12 hours.</p>

    <h2 id="s12">How conditions shape each day</h2>
    <p>No two days of a heli week are quite the same, because the mountains change daily. The <strong>snowpack, the weather and the light</strong> all shift, and the guide reshapes the day around them, choosing different zones, aspects and lines to find the best skiing on offer. This is why the week feels alive rather than scripted.</p>
    <p>Early in the week the guide learns your group; later, with conditions understood and legs warmed up, the skiing often gets better and better. A cold, clear day might mean deep powder on shaded aspects; a warmer, sunny one might mean soft spring corn on the lower pitches. Reading these changes is exactly what an IFMGA-certified guide is for, and it is part of what makes each day of the week distinct and rewarding.</p>

    <h2 id="s13">What to do with your evenings</h2>
    <p>Evenings on a heli week are unhurried and comfortable, and how you spend them is part of the pleasure. Most guests settle into a gentle routine: a proper dinner at the <strong>Sigló Hótel</strong>, a soak in the harbour-side hot tub to ease tired legs, and the next day's briefing before an early night.</p>
    <p>Siglufjörður itself rewards a wander, a former herring town with real character, a celebrated museum, cafés and the drama of the fjord all around. In late season the long daylight makes evening strolls under a low golden sun a highlight in themselves. It is the balance of hard days and easy nights that makes a heli week feel restorative rather than exhausting. Our <a href="/field-notes/siglufjordur-travel-guide">Siglufjörður travel guide</a> has ideas.</p>

